# Line-ending policy.
#
# Without this file the repository's contents depend on each contributor's
# `core.autocrlf`, and a machine configured differently can commit CRLF into
# the index. Every text blob here is LF today (`git ls-files --eol`); these
# rules are what keep it that way independently of local git config.
#
# `text=auto` normalises on commit only — worktree endings still follow each
# machine's autocrlf, so this changes nothing on disk for existing checkouts.
* text=auto

# Read by a POSIX shell, so these must be LF in the *worktree* too, not just in
# the index. A CRLF shebang resolves to the interpreter name plus a carriage
# return: `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which fails as "bad interpreter" on Linux and
# macOS. Git Bash on Windows happens to tolerate it, which is exactly why this
# stays invisible on a Windows checkout until someone runs install-cli.sh under
# WSL or on a clean VM.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compiled into the binary with `include_str!` and then parsed byte-for-byte:
# the frontmatter delimiter is matched as `---\n` and each fenced command is
# split on `\n`, so a CRLF worktree yields `---\r\n` and argv entries with a
# trailing `\r`. That is not a checkout detail by the time it is embedded — it
# is the program's own data, and it broke four `skills` tests on the Windows
# runner while every other platform passed. Same reasoning as `*.sh` above:
# `text=auto` alone normalises the index, not the worktree the compiler reads.
docs/skills/*.md text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
*.tiff binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
